<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Blemmyes</h1><p class="st-meta">250 CE – 550 CE (approx.) · capital Kalabsha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Nomadic people of the Eastern Desert, ancestors of the Beja, who raided Roman Egypt and held Lower Nubia in late antiquity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Blemmyes lived in the desert hills between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and are generally identified as ancestors of the modern Beja. Greek geographers mention them from the 3rd century BCE, and from the 3rd century CE Roman sources record them raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> and the trade routes to the Red Sea ports.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They were organised in tribes under kings and phylarchs, and in the 5th century held parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalabsha">Talmis</a> (Kalabsha) as a centre. In 298 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> withdrew the frontier to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a> and paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> to both the Blemmyes and the Nobadians. The Blemmyes worshipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> and had the right to borrow her statue from Philae. Letters written in Greek, such as one from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phonen">Phonen</a>, survive at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qasr-ibrim">Qasr Ibrim</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 5th to 6th centuries the Christian kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nobadia">Nobadia</a> drove them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silko">King Silko</a> recorded his victories at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalabsha">Kalabsha</a>. The closure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a> temple under Justinian around 537 ended their religious tie with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and their descendants remained in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-desert">Eastern Desert</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/blemmyes.jpg" alt="Blemmyes" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Dravinia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blemmyes_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>250 CE – 550 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kalabsha</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Phonen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Egyptian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Blemmyan (early Beja), Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of the Blemmyes, Beja</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=400&amp;focus=blemmyes" aria-label="Blemmyes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5550 1310 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Blemmyes lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5870 1566L5843 1614"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5843 1614h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5870 1566h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5870 1566h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kalabsha. <a href="/atlas?year=400&amp;focus=blemmyes">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

